p.src:
For many degrees of freedom, the same cancellation occurs for every canonical pair: $\begin{aligned}
  \sum_i\left(
    \frac{\partial\dot p_i}{\partial p_i}
    +\frac{\partial\dot q_i}{\partial q_i}
  \right)
  &=
  \sum_i\left(
    -\frac{\partial^2H}{\partial p_i\,\partial q_i}
    +\frac{\partial^2H}{\partial q_i\,\partial p_i}
  \right)\\
  &=0.
  \end{aligned}$ Stretching in one canonical direction is balanced by compression in its conjugate direction. This pairwise cancellation is stronger structure than a coincidental cancellation among unrelated axes.

